<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shalmaneser V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned 727–722 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shalmaneser V was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from 727 to 722 BCE, son and successor of Tiglath-Pileser III. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshea">Hoshea of Israel</a> stopped paying tribute, he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, which fell around 722 BCE, ending the northern kingdom. He died or was overthrown at about that time, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> claimed the conquest and deported much of Israel's population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-v.jpg" alt="Shalmaneser V" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shalmaneser_V_crown_prince.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shalmaneser V was the son and heir of Tiglath-Pileser III. His name, Salmānu-ašarēd in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, means Salmānu is foremost, and it was a regnal name borne by four earlier Assyrian kings. Some later sources, among them the Ptolemaic Canon and the Babylonian King List, call him Ululayu, which was possibly his birth name.</p><p>Letters sent to his father while he was crown prince, signed Ululayu, report that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and its forts are well. They show him detaining emissaries and moving goods, and his duties appear to have been mostly diplomatic or concerned with the palace household. Two Assyriologists have suggested that he acted as regent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> while his father was on campaign, though they note this is speculative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Shalmaneser appears to have become king of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> without challenge, although he faced some resistance from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a> in the south. Few contemporary sources survive. Apart from inscriptions on lion-shaped weights, no commemorative inscription can be attributed to him with confidence, and he built little in the great cities of the Assyrian heartland, so he probably spent much of his short reign at war. He held the office of eponym in his fourth year rather than his second, a change that later kings seem to have respected.</p><p>He campaigned widely in the west, against Phoenician cities and against the kingdom of Tabal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. The Greek historian Menander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> records a five-year siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, probably in his reign. His best-known undertaking was the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, which ended the kingdom and made it the Assyrian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samerina">Samerina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a> and the Bible ascribe the conquest to Shalmaneser, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> claims it in his own inscriptions. One reconstruction places the start of the siege in 725 or 724 BCE and its end in 722 BCE, near the end of Shalmaneser's reign. Nearly thirty thousand people were deported, and Sargon took responsibility for the resettlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shalmaneser was deposed in 722 BCE and replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>. The circumstances are unclear but were probably violent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a> says only that he met the fate. A single inscription of Sargon portrays him as a godless tyrant who deprived the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> of its privileges, but this serves chiefly to justify Sargon's own rise.</p><p>Sargon's early years saw opposition from a significant faction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, over six thousand of whom he resettled as guilty. It is unlikely that Sargon was a legitimate heir, and he may have been unrelated to Shalmaneser, in which case Shalmaneser was the last king of the Adaside dynasty, which had ruled Assyria for almost a thousand years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shalmaneser V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shalmaneser V</b> besieged Samaria, which fell in 722 BCE after a siege the Bible puts at three years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
